A much simpler variant seems to be just to add

```C#
partial class CborReader
{
    public void Reset(ReadOnlyMemory<byte> data);
}
```

And not bother letting the two options change, or ever have a state of having unassigned data.  For your described scenario it'd be

```C#
CborReader reader = null;

foreach (var record in records)
{
    if (reader is null)
    {
        reader = new CborReader(record.Cbor);
    }
    else
    {
        reader.Reset(record.Cbor);
    }

    ...
}
```

or just empty-initialize the reader, if you want to get rid of the branch.

```C#
CborReader reader = new CborReader(ReadOnlyMemory<byte>.Empty);

foreach (var record in records)
{
    reader.Reset(record.Cbor);
    ...
}
```

As-proposed, there's ambiguity.  What happens if you're in deep state and call SetBuffer?  What does Reset do without a corresponding call to SetBuffer?  And, of course, why bother tracking a state that says that there's no associated data?

[Video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xsWMkQ5QNjM&t=0h6m42s)

Looks good as proposed

```C#
namespace System.Formats.Cbor;

partial class CborReader
{
    //resets reader state
    void Reset(ReadOnlyMemory<byte> data);
}
```

